<h1>how do i prove i can code if my github is empty and i have no internship</h1>

<p class="answer-capsule">recruiters stopped trusting empty repositories years ago. the real signal is shipped work someone else had to use: a tool that solved a real bug, a contribution merged into code people depend on, or a live system you can walk through, because degrees and green github squares are theater while proof is work with consequences.</p>

<div class="tldr">
<ul>
<li>resume use among employers fell to <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">67%</a> in 2025, down from 73%, as hiring managers hunt for work samples they can actually evaluate</li>
<li><a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">85% of employers</a> now use skills-based hiring, up from 81% the year before, meaning your proof of work matters more than your pedigree</li>
<li>an internship cuts underemployment odds by <a href="https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/6777c52f82e5471a3732ea25/679a6fadfda4220bbac585d7_Talent-Disrupted-2.pdf">48.5%</a>, but it is not the only path: live contributions, technical writing, and scored simulations create the same validated signal</li>
<li>big tech new-grad hiring is down <a href="https://www.signalfire.com/blog/signalfire-state-of-talent-report-2025">more than 50%</a> from 2019, so the old pipeline of "prestige internship → return offer" is broken for most; you need a direct proof channel instead</li>
</ul>
</div>

<section>
<h2>why github stars and internship logos lost their power</h2>

<p>the old developer credibility stack was simple: prestigious cs program, google internship, green github heatmap. each piece signaled "someone else already vetted this person." but that stack collapsed because everyone gamed it.</p>

<p>github is the worst offender. recruiters know that a commit graph can be manufactured: auto-commit bots, forked tutorials passed off as original work, leetcode solutions pushed daily to paint the grid green. the signal became noise.</p>

<p>internships followed the same arc. when big tech new-grad hiring dropped <a href="https://www.signalfire.com/blog/signalfire-state-of-talent-report-2025">more than 50%</a> from 2019 peaks, the internship funnel narrowed dramatically. the remaining slots concentrated at target schools, so the credential became a filter for wealth and geography, not ability. worse, many "software engineering internships" never touch production: interns build internal dashboards that get shelved, or shadow engineers in meetings. the logo on your resume says little about whether you can ship under pressure.</p>

<p>the deeper problem is that both signals are static. a github repo is frozen code. an internship is a past event. neither shows how you think through ambiguity, respond to feedback, or adapt when your first approach fails. modern software work is collaborative and iterative, so recruiters now hunt for evidence of that dynamic process.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>the three proof paths that actually convert</h2>

<p>if traditional signals are dead, what replaces them? three channels create verifiable, hard-to-fake evidence that you can build real things.</p>

<h3>path one: merged contributions to depended-upon code</h3>

<p>find an open-source project people actually use. not a tutorial library with twelve stars: a dependency that other projects import, a tool with active issue queues and release cycles. start by reproducing a confirmed bug. document your reproduction steps in the issue. then submit a pull request that fixes it, with tests.</p>

<p>the key is "depended-upon." a merged pr to react, rust's cargo, or a popular cli tool carries weight because maintainers protect their reputation. they will not merge garbage. your contribution becomes part of infrastructure others rely on, which means it had to survive real review standards. when a recruiter asks "tell me about a time you had to refactor for performance," you point to a specific commit with benchmark numbers that a maintainer accepted.</p>

<p>start small: documentation fixes, test coverage gaps, dependency updates. these teach you the project's conventions and build trust with maintainers. within a month you can graduate to substantive bugs. track everything in a brag document: issue links, pr merges, performance improvements, security patches. this is your replacement portfolio.</p>

<h3>path two: technical writing that exposes your reasoning</h3>

<p>explain a bug you hunted for six hours. walk through a failed architecture decision and what you learned. publish on your own domain, or on platforms where practitioners actually read. the act of explanation forces clarity, and clear explanation is a job skill: senior engineers spend more time communicating than coding.</p>

<p>the best technical writing includes runnable code. not snippets: full reproductions. a post that says "here is a race condition i found in this async runtime" with a github repository that demonstrates it, plus your fix branch, proves you can isolate, reproduce, and solve. compare that to a leetcode medium: one shows engineering judgment, the other shows you can memorize patterns.</p>

<p>writing also creates discoverability. recruiters search for specific technologies. a deep dive into "how we reduced p99 latency by 40% using io_uring" ranks for terms that match job descriptions. your proof becomes a magnet, not a pitch.</p>

<h3>path three: live systems with real users or real stakes</h3>

<p>build something that handles actual traffic, actual money, or actual complaints. a browser extension with 500 real users and a one-star review you addressed. a script that automates a workflow for your family's business, deployed on a server you manage, with monitoring that pages you at 2am when it breaks. a small saas with ten paying customers and a churned customer you interviewed to understand why.</p>

<p>the "real stakes" part matters. a todo app tutorial has no consequences. a service that loses data because you skipped backups teaches you operational rigor the hard way. that scar tissue is valuable. when a recruiter asks about handling incidents, you describe the pagerduty rotation you built for yourself after the database incident.</p>

<p>document the system architecture, the failure modes you considered, the tradeoffs you made. this is not a readme: it is a post-mortem and a design doc combined. it shows you think like an engineer, not a tutorial follower.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>how to present proof when you have no traditional credentials</h2>

<p>once you have evidence, you must frame it. recruiters scan fast, so lead with the consequence, not the activity.</p>

<p>bad: "contributed to open source." good: "fixed memory leak in x library used by 12,000 projects; reduced rss by 30%." bad: "wrote technical blog posts." good: "documented distributed systems failure mode that became reference for y framework's official docs." bad: "built side projects." good: "runs telemetry pipeline processing 2m events daily for local nonprofit; self-managed migration from heroku to vps cutting costs 70%."</p>

<p>the formula is: what you built, who used it, what metric moved, what you learned when it broke. this applies to cold outreach, cover letters, and interview stories. never apologize for missing credentials. the entire point of proof-based hiring is that credentials are a proxy you no longer need.</p>

<p>for structured evaluation, seek scored environments. platforms that simulate production incidents, code review scenarios, or architecture discussions against a professional bar give you a third-party validated score. this is not a certificate: it is a calibrated measurement of how your work compares to practicing engineers. <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">85% of employers</a> now use skills-based hiring, so a score from a rigorous simulation travels farther than a bootcamp completion badge.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>what to avoid: fake signals that waste your time</h2>

<p>some replacements for github and internships are themselves theater. learn to spot them.</p>

<p>leetcode grind without narrative: solving 300 problems proves endurance, not engineering. if you must do competitive programming, frame it as "i optimized this specific algorithm for this specific constraint," not as a volume metric.</p>

<p>tutorial projects presented as original: a netflix clone from a udemy course, a twitter clone from a bootcamp. recruiters have seen these hundreds of times. if you built one, extend it: add real-time features, deploy it to handle load, write the auth system yourself instead of using the tutorial's firebase drop-in. the gap between following a tutorial and shipping your own extension is where your proof lives.</p>

<p>certificates from course platforms: <a href="https://www.coursera.org/about/">168 million registered learners</a> on coursera alone means completion badges are not differentiators. a coursera certificate is worthless noise. the only value is what you build after, and you can build that without paying for the credential.</p>

<p>hackathon projects without follow-through: a demo that worked on stage and never again. if you hackathon, ship the project to actual users afterward, or open-source the component that solved a general problem. the weekend is not the proof. the month after, when you debug real issues, is.</p>
</section>

<section>
<h2>your 90-day plan to build credible proof</h2>

<p>week 1-2: audit your skills honestly. list languages and frameworks you have used for non-tutorial projects. identify one area where you could contribute to a real codebase: a python library you use, a javascript tool in your toolchain, a rust crate you depend on.</p>

<p>week 3-4: make your first merged contribution. aim low: fix a typo in docs, add a test for an untested function, update a deprecated api call. the goal is learning the project's contribution flow, not impressing anyone.</p>

<p>week 5-8: graduate to a substantive bug or small feature. document your approach in a technical post. include benchmarks, tradeoff analysis, what you tried that failed. publish it.</p>

<p>week 9-12: build or extend a live system with real users. if you have no ideas, solve your own pain: a tool for a hobby, automation for a community you are in, a better interface for a public dataset. deploy it, monitor it, iterate based on actual usage.</p>

<p>by day 90, you have: one merged contribution with review history, one technical post with runnable code, one live system with user feedback. this replaces an empty github and a missing internship with evidence that is harder to fake and more informative to evaluate.</p>
</section>

<section class="faq">
<h2>frequently asked questions</h2>

<h3>do i need to know the project's entire codebase before contributing?</h3>
<p>no. maintainers do not expect omniscience. start with issues tagged "good first issue" or "help wanted." read enough to understand the specific module you are touching. ask clarifying questions in the issue thread: maintainers prefer engaged contributors who communicate over silent perfectionists who never submit.</p>

<h3>what if my code gets rejected?</h3>
<p>rejection is the signal working. a rejected pr with detailed feedback is more valuable than a merged pr to a dead project. incorporate the feedback, resubmit, and document what you learned. this cycle, iterated, is what professional engineering looks like.</p>

<h3>can i use freelance work as proof?</h3>
<p>yes, if you can show the work and its impact. "built website for client" is weak. "reduced client's checkout abandonment 15% by rebuilding payment flow with proper error handling, documented in this case study" is strong. the constraint is usually client confidentiality, so negotiate portfolio rights upfront or build anonymized case studies.</p>

<h3>how do i compete with candidates who have both credentials and proof?</h3>
<p>you do not compete on their terrain. credentials are a proxy for proof, and proxies decay. focus on demonstrating judgment under uncertainty, which is what senior roles actually require. a candidate who shipped under constraints, debugged live failures, and iterated based on feedback often outperforms one who collected badges smoothly.</p>

<h3>is a bootcamp a viable alternative path?</h3>
<p>bootcamps teach syntax and frameworks quickly, but the average cost is roughly <a href="https://www.coursereport.com/coding-bootcamp-ultimate-guide">$13,584</a> and outcomes vary widely. the value is in structured practice and cohort pressure, not the certificate. if you choose a bootcamp, treat it as time to build live projects with real users, not as a credential acquisition process. audited placement data from specific programs is available through <a href="https://www.cirr.org/schooldata">cirr</a>.</p>
</section>

